{"id":"h013","run":2,"role":"test_wrong","category":"science-chemistry","question":"Calculate the magnetic moment (μI) of a 13C nucleus.\nIs the correct answer \"9.1445 x 10^-27 J T-1\"?","truth":"yes","model_answer":"no","correct":false,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"No standard formula or tabulated constant for 13C gives 9.1445 × 10^-27 J/T.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The 13C nucleus has spin I = 1/2.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.2,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The measured magnetic moment of 13C is about 0.7024 nuclear magnetons.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The nuclear magneton is about 5.051 × 10^-27 J/T.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.4923,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The measured moment corresponds to about 3.55 × 10^-27 J/T.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Using gamma and sqrt(I(I+1)) gives about 6.1 × 10^-27 J/T.","confidence":0.8,"lean":-0.4,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The mismatch of the gamma-based value with the tabulated moment is expected because sqrt(I(I+1)) gives the angular momentum magnitude, not the z-component.","confidence":0.8,"lean":0.0,"theta":1.2566,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"Neither approach yields 9.14 × 10^-27 J/T.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.8,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416},{"text":"The proposed value is about 2.6 times larger than the established measured moment, which decides the matter.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4137,"phi":3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":7,"J":0.8,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":8,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":2,"J":0.3,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":5,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":1,"j":6,"J":0.4,"relation":"supports"},{"i":2,"j":3,"J":0.5,"relation